{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1784646302,"nonce":"C59/QQw4aho1B9qN/ZcW615oA1Y=","message":"Root-cause analysis (no fix proposed).\n\nThis is a **liblore version mismatch**, not a Textual issue. The full traceback\nin this thread ends at:\n\n    AttributeError: 'LoreNode' object has no attribute 'reset_cancel'\n    src/b4/review_tui/_common.py:238  -\u003e  b4.get_lore_node().reset_cancel()\n\nPath: `UpdateAllScreen.on_mount` (`_modals.py:2781`) -\u003e `run_lore_worker`\n(`_common.py:266`) -\u003e `with lore_request():`, whose `__enter__`\n(`_common.py:238`) calls `LoreNode.reset_cancel()`. The exception is raised\nsynchronously on the main thread during `on_mount`, *before* the worker starts,\nso `exit_on_error=False` (which only tames worker-thread failures) never comes\ninto play and the screen crashes outright.\n\n`reset_cancel()` was added to liblore for the reset-on-enter chokepoint (b4\ncommit bd3621e); the installed/pinned liblore predates it. That is exactly why\nupdating the requirements files cleared it for the reporter, and why the same\ncrash also hit the `update` and `review` actions -- they all funnel through\n`run_lore_worker` / `lore_request`. (Confirmed locally: installed Textual 8.2.4\naccepts `run_worker(thread=...)`, so the kwargs are fine; the liblore pin lags\nthe code.)\n\nSeparately, the reporter's note that archived-then-removed `b4/review/*`\nbranches still appear in the list is a different issue -\u003e 784a8f8 (b4 tui: Old\nserieses not removed).","files":null}]}